  "advice": {
    "breakdown": {
      "meaning": "The same critical asset stops again and again: each breakdown costs the minutes of the stop and the parcels behind it. Repairs fix the symptom; the cause is still there.",
      "moves": ["Take the latest breakdown to 5 Whys with the technician who fixed it, while the details are fresh.", "Look at the plan of this asset: is a task missing, too rare or done late before the stops?", "Agree one countermeasure with an owner and a date, and check the asset for 30 days after it."],
      "question": "What did we see in the days before the last stop, and who saw it first?"
    },
    "overdue": {
      "meaning": "A task on a critical asset is past its date. Every day it waits, the risk of a stop or of a failed inspection grows, and a statutory check that is overdue can mean the asset must not be used.",
      "moves": ["Book the task this week: a slot, a person or the supplier, and the parts.", "If it is a statutory check, decide today whether the asset may run until then, and write the decision down.", "Find out why it slipped: no slot, no supplier date, nobody owning it, and fix that too."],
      "question": "Who owns this task, and what stopped it from being done on its date?"
    },
    "none": {
      "meaning": "Nothing critical is overdue and no critical asset breaks down again and again. Keep the rhythm.",
      "moves": ["Print the work list on Monday and give each task its owner.", "Log every task the day it is done, with a photo where it matters.", "Log every breakdown, short ones too: they show where the plan needs a task."],
      "question": "Which asset would hurt us most if it stopped today, and is its plan right?"
    }
  },
  "tips": [
    ["One plan for every asset.", "Add each asset with its tag, area and criticality, and take the typical tasks of its type. Set the date each task was last done, so the first due dates are right."],
    ["Dates or hours.", "A task is due after days, weeks, months or years, or after operating hours, whichever comes first. Log meter readings and the planner moves the date by the real use."],
    ["Log the day it is done.", "Done, postponed with a reason, or a breakdown with its downtime: each entry moves the plan. Scan the tag on the asset to pick it at once."],
    ["Compliance, honestly.", "A task counts as on time when it is done by its due date, or within a tenth of its interval (at most 7 days). A postponed task that is done late still counts as late."],
    ["A for critical.", "Overdue tasks on A assets and repeated breakdowns go to the Action Tracker; the causes go to Pareto, one breakdown at a time to 5 Whys."]
  ],
